<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">banter</ansi>

Your companions are not silent. At camp, and now and then after a won
fight, two or three of them trade a line or two, so the company feels like
comrades. It is only talk: it changes no numbers, costs nothing, and never
moves the clock.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">When they talk</ansi>

  as a camp rest begins   always (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>)
  as a camp rest ends     about one rest in two
  after a won battle      about one battle in three, never in the middle of one

A flight, a defeat, or a broken-off fight draws no banter.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What they say</ansi>

Each companion has a personality rolled when it joins your company, and it keeps
it: stoic, cheerful, grim, boastful, wry or devout. What a companion says
also follows its archetype (a warrior talks about shields, a wizard about
spells) and its alignment, good, neutral or evil (<ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>). Some
lines are said in answer to another companion's, and some mention another
member, or you, by name. Talk of the stars, the moon or the night waits
for nightfall on the world clock.

Talk reads like a page of a story, not a log: sometimes the quote comes first,
sometimes a small gesture does, sometimes the speaker's name sits in the
middle of the line. Two companions never say the same words in one go. When a
choice of yours shifts a companion's loyalty (<ansi fg="command">help opinions</ansi>), the change
sits quietly in grey at the end of the line.

After a battle the talk follows how it went:

  a close call    someone was left at under a third of their health
  a fall          a companion was killed in the fight; the others want
                  them raised in time (<ansi fg="command">help resurrect</ansi>)
  a clean win     nobody was hurt much
  anything else   a plain victory

A companion does not repeat a line it said lately, so the talk stays fresh.
Only companions who are with you can speak, and it takes at least two.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Turning it off</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">set banter off</ansi>   no more camp or battle talk
  <ansi fg="command">set banter on</ansi>    bring it back (it is on by default)
  <ansi fg="command">set banter</ansi>       flip it; <ansi fg="command">set</ansi> shows where it stands

In the web client, Company > Camp shows the company's latest talk under
"Around the fire" (<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

A personality also decides what a companion thinks of your choices: mercy,
camps, sharing food, relics and the choices in scenes on the road
(<ansi fg="command">help opinions</ansi>).

Friends and rivals sometimes talk about each other, and it nudges their
bond (<ansi fg="command">help bonds</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help bonds</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help set</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help opinions</ansi>.
